(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["mth-11-08"] = {"mth-11-08-m01":{"idea":"A function is a rule with one clear output for every input. It never gives two outputs for the same input.","steps":["Take a function from A to B.","Every element of A must be used as an input.","Each of them is sent to exactly one element of B.","Example: x = y² is not a function, because x = 4 gives y = 2 and y = −2."],"others":"Having no output for some input breaks the rule that every input is used. Having at least one or two outputs allows more than one output, which a function does not allow.","trap":"Exactly one output: not zero, not two.","answer":"exactly one element of B"},"mth-11-08-m02":{"idea":"A function has three sets to know: domain (inputs), co-domain (the target set) and range (outputs actually reached).","steps":["The domain is A, the set of all inputs.","The co-domain is B, the set where outputs are allowed to lie.","The range is the set of values f(x) that really occur.","So the range is a part of the co-domain."],"others":"The other orders mix up the three sets, for example calling B the domain. The range is never the set of all x values, since those are inputs.","trap":"Range is the part of B that is actually used.","answer":"A, B, and the set of the values f(x) that actually occur"},"mth-11-08-m03":{"idea":"A square root needs a number that is zero or positive under it. This gives the domain.","steps":["The expression under the root is x − 2.","It must not be negative: x − 2 ≥ 0.","So x ≥ 2.","At x = 2, f = √0 = 0, which is fine, so 2 is included."],"others":"All real numbers is false, since x = 0 gives √(−2). The choice x ≤ 2 reverses the sign. The choice x > 2 only leaves out x = 2, which is allowed.","trap":"Square root allows zero. A log does not.","answer":"x ≥ 2"},"mth-11-08-m04":{"idea":"A fraction is not defined where its denominator is zero. So we find those x values and remove them.","steps":["Set the denominator to zero: x² − 9 = 0.","So x² = 9 and x = 3 or x = −3.","These two values are removed.","The domain is all real numbers except 3 and −3."],"others":"Removing only 3 forgets that −3 also makes the denominator zero. The choice x > 3 cuts away too many values. The choice that removes 9 mixes up x and x².","trap":"Both roots of x² = 9 must be removed.","answer":"all real numbers except 3 and −3"},"mth-11-08-m05":{"idea":"A logarithm is defined only for positive numbers. This sets the domain.","steps":["The input of the log is x − 1.","It must be positive: x − 1 > 0.","So x > 1.","At x = 1, the log of 0 is undefined, so 1 is not included."],"others":"The choice x ≥ 1 includes x = 1, where log 0 does not exist. The choice x < 1 reverses the sign. All real numbers would let the log take zero or negative inputs.","trap":"Log needs strictly positive input, so no equals sign.","answer":"x > 1"},"mth-11-08-m06":{"idea":"To find the range of a quadratic, complete the square. This shows the smallest value.","steps":["Write x² − 4x = (x − 2)² − 4.","So f(x) = (x − 2)² − 4 + 7 = (x − 2)² + 3.","A square is at least 0, so f(x) ≥ 3, with equality at x = 2.","Values grow without limit, so the range is [3, ∞)."],"others":"The range [7, ∞) takes the constant 7 as the smallest value, but f(2) = 3 is smaller. The range (−∞, 3] reverses the direction. All real numbers is false because f never goes below 3.","trap":"Do not read the answer from the constant term alone.","answer":"[3, ∞)"},"mth-11-08-m07":{"idea":"One-one means no two different inputs share an output.","steps":["Suppose f(x₁) = f(x₂).","For a one-one function, this forces x₁ = x₂.","Same idea: different inputs always give different outputs.","Example: 3x − 2 is one-one, but x² is not, since 2 and −2 both give 4."],"others":"Every element of the co-domain being an output is the meaning of onto. Domain equal to range does not decide one-one. Every input giving the same output is a constant function.","trap":"One-one is about inputs. Onto is about outputs.","answer":"different inputs always give different outputs"},"mth-11-08-m08":{"idea":"Onto means the function reaches every element of the co-domain.","steps":["For each y in B, there is some x in A with f(x) = y.","So the range equals the co-domain.","Whether it is onto depends on the co-domain chosen.","Example: x² is onto [0, ∞), but not onto all real numbers."],"others":"Every element of A having a different output is the meaning of one-one. A constant function reaches only one value. Having fewer elements in the domain makes onto impossible for finite sets.","answer":"every element of B is the output of at least one input"},"mth-11-08-m09":{"idea":"A function that is both one-one and onto is called a bijection. Only bijections have inverses.","steps":["One-one makes each output come from exactly one input.","Onto makes sure every element of the co-domain is reached.","Together, the rule can be reversed: each output goes back to its own input.","So a bijection has an inverse, and no other function has."],"others":"The word identity is a special bijection, which does have an inverse. A polynomial may or may not be a bijection. A constant function is not one-one, so it has no inverse.","answer":"a bijection, and only such a function has an inverse"},"mth-11-08-m10":{"idea":"For one-one functions, each element of the first set must have its own, different image. Count the choices one by one.","steps":["The first element can go to any of the 5 elements.","The second must go to a different one: 4 choices.","The third has 3 choices left.","Total = 5 × 4 × 3 = 60 (this is 5P3)."],"others":"The value 125 is 5³, which counts all functions. The value 243 is 3⁵, with the sets swapped. The value 10 is 5C3, which ignores which element goes where.","trap":"All functions: nᵐ. One-one: nPm.","answer":"60"},"mth-11-08-m11":{"idea":"Each element of the first set picks its image on its own. Multiply the number of choices.","steps":["Each of the 3 elements has 2 possible images.","The choices are independent.","So the number of functions = 2 × 2 × 2 = 2³ = 8.","In general, from m elements to n elements: nᵐ."],"others":"The value 6 is 3 × 2, which multiplies the two sizes and is not the counting rule. The value 9 is 3², with the sets swapped. The value 5 is 3 + 2, which is not a product.","trap":"Power: (target size)^(source size), not the other way.","answer":"8"},"mth-11-08-m12":{"idea":"Count all functions, then remove the ones that fail to be onto.","steps":["All functions from 4 elements to 2 elements: 2⁴ = 16.","A function is not onto if it uses only one of the 2 elements.","That happens in 2 cases: all go to the first, or all go to the second.","Onto functions = 16 − 2 = 14."],"others":"The value 16 counts all functions, including the two that are not onto. The value 8 is 2³, with a wrong power. The value 12 removes too many.","trap":"Subtract the constant functions.","answer":"14"},"mth-11-08-m13":{"idea":"In (g∘f)(x), the function written closest to x works first.","steps":["(g∘f)(x) means g(f(x)).","So f is applied to x first.","Then g is applied to the result.","In general, g∘f and f∘g are different."],"others":"The choice f(g(x)) reverses the order. The choices f(x) × g(x) and f(x) + g(x) are product and sum of functions, not composition.","trap":"Read composition from right to left.","answer":"g(f(x)), which means f is applied first"},"mth-11-08-m14":{"idea":"For a composite function, work from the inside. First f, then g.","steps":["(g∘f)(2) = g(f(2)).","f(2) = 2 + 1 = 3.","g(3) = 3² = 9."],"others":"The value 5 is f(g(2)) = f(4), the other order. The value 7 comes from 2² + 3, a wrong mix. The value 4 is g(2) alone.","trap":"Check the order: g∘f is f first.","answer":"9"},"mth-11-08-m15":{"idea":"To find an inverse, write y = f(x), solve for x, and then swap the names x and y.","steps":["Put y = 3x − 2.","Add 2: y + 2 = 3x.","Divide by 3: x = (y + 2)/3.","Swap the names: f⁻¹(x) = (x + 2)/3.","Check: f(f⁻¹(x)) = 3 × (x + 2)/3 − 2 = x."],"others":"The form (x − 2)/3 has the wrong sign. The form 3x + 2 undoes only the subtraction. The form 1/(3x − 2) is the reciprocal, not the inverse.","trap":"f⁻¹ is not 1/f.","answer":"f⁻¹(x) = (x + 2)/3"},"mth-11-08-m16":{"idea":"The vertical line test checks that each input has one output.","steps":["A function gives only one y for each x.","A vertical line meets the graph at all points with the same x.","So no vertical line may cut the graph at more than one point.","The horizontal line test is a different test, for one-one."],"others":"Passing through the origin is not needed, since f(x) = x + 1 does not. Being a straight line is not needed, since x² is a function. The horizontal line test checks one-one, not whether it is a function.","trap":"Vertical: function. Horizontal: one-one.","answer":"no vertical line meets it at more than one point"},"mth-11-08-m17":{"idea":"A constant function gives the same output for every input.","steps":["f(x) = 5 can be evaluated for any real x.","So the domain is all real numbers.","Every output is 5.","So the range is {5}, one value only."],"others":"The domain x ≥ 5 puts a wrong limit on the input. The choice with domain {5} and range all real numbers swaps the two sets. The last choice claims every real number is an output, but only 5 is.","answer":"all real numbers, and its range is {5}"},"mth-11-08-m18":{"idea":"eˣ is always positive. It is never zero and never negative.","steps":["For large positive x, eˣ becomes very large.","For large negative x, eˣ comes close to 0.","But it never reaches 0.","So the range is (0, ∞), the positive real numbers."],"others":"The range [0, ∞) wrongly includes 0, which eˣ never reaches. All real numbers is false, since eˣ is never negative. The range (−∞, 0) is the opposite side.","trap":"0 is approached but not reached.","answer":"(0, ∞), the positive real numbers"},"mth-11-08-m19":{"idea":"The absolute value gives the distance from zero. So it is never negative.","steps":["For x ≥ 0, |x| = x.","For x < 0, |x| = −x, which is positive.","Example: |−5| = 5 and |3| = 3.","So it is a function in two pieces."],"others":"The rule x for all x gives negative outputs for negative x. The rule −x for all x gives negative outputs for positive x. The rule with 0 for x < 0 is not the absolute value.","answer":"x if x ≥ 0, and −x if x < 0"},"mth-11-08-m20":{"idea":"The greatest integer [x] is the largest integer that is less than or equal to x.","steps":["Look at x = −2.3.","Integers not above −2.3 are −3, −4, −5, and so on.","The greatest of them is −3.","So [−2.3] = −3."],"others":"The value −2 is above −2.3, so it is too big. The value −2.3 is not an integer. The value 2 has the wrong sign. Do not just drop the decimal part.","trap":"For negative numbers, [x] goes down, not toward zero.","answer":"−3"},"mth-11-08-m21":{"idea":"The signum function gives only the sign of x.","steps":["If x > 0, then sgn(x) = 1.","If x = 0, then sgn(x) = 0.","If x < 0, then sgn(x) = −1.","Example: sgn(−7) = −1."],"others":"The rule with 1 for x ≥ 0 gives 1 at zero, but sgn(0) = 0. The rules x and |x| return the size of x, not just its sign.","trap":"The value at zero is 0.","answer":"1 for x > 0, 0 for x = 0, and −1 for x < 0"},"mth-11-08-m22":{"idea":"Put x = −2 into each part. Then add.","steps":["|x − 3| = |−2 − 3| = |−5| = 5.","sgn(x − 1) = sgn(−3) = −1.","f(−2) = 5 + (−1) = 4."],"others":"The value 6 treats the sign part as +1. The value 5 forgets the sign part. The value −4 changes the sign of the first part.","trap":"sgn of a negative number is −1.","answer":"4"},"mth-11-08-n01":{"idea":"In a composite function, work from the inside out.","steps":["(g∘f)(2) = g(f(2)).","f(2) = 2 + 1 = 3.","g(3) = 3² = 9."],"trap":"Check: (g∘f)(x) = (x + 1)², and at x = 2 it is 9.","answer":"9"},"mth-11-08-n02":{"idea":"Find the conditions for the function to be defined. Then count the integers that satisfy them.","steps":["The square root needs x − 2 ≥ 0, so x ≥ 2.","The denominator needs x − 5 ≠ 0, so x ≠ 5.","Integers from 2 to 10: 2, 3, ..., 10, which is 9 numbers.","Remove 5: 9 − 1 = 8."],"trap":"Do not forget to count x = 2, which makes the root zero but is allowed.","answer":"8"},"mth-11-08-n03":{"idea":"Complete the square to see the least value of the quadratic.","steps":["f(x) = x² − 4x + 7 = (x − 2)² + 3.","A square is at least 0, so f(x) ≥ 3.","The least value is reached at x = 2.","Least value = 3.000.","Formula check: c − b²/(4a) = 7 − 16/4 = 3."],"answer":"3.000"},"mth-11-08-n04":{"idea":"f⁻¹(7) is the input x that gives output 7. Solve 3x − 2 = 7.","steps":["Set 3x − 2 = 7.","Add 2: 3x = 9.","Divide by 3: x = 3.","So f⁻¹(7) = 3.000."],"trap":"Check: f(3) = 9 − 2 = 7.","answer":"3.000"},"mth-11-08-n05":{"idea":"One-one functions need a different image for each element. Count the choices in turn.","steps":["The first element has 5 choices.","The second has 4 choices, a different one.","The third has 3 choices.","Total = 5 × 4 × 3 = 60."],"trap":"This is 5P3. All functions would be 5³ = 125.","answer":"60"},"mth-11-08-n06":{"idea":"Count all functions, then subtract those that are not onto.","steps":["All functions from 4 elements to 2 elements: 2⁴ = 16.","The functions that are not onto send everything to one element.","There are 2 such functions.","Onto functions = 16 − 2 = 14."],"answer":"14"},"mth-11-08-n07":{"idea":"Put x = −2 into each part and add.","steps":["|x − 3| = |−2 − 3| = |−5| = 5.","sgn(x − 1) = sgn(−3) = −1.","f(−2) = 5 + (−1) = 4."],"trap":"The signum of a negative number is −1, not +1.","answer":"4"},"mth-11-08-n08":{"idea":"Apply f twice. First find f(1), then apply f to that result.","steps":["f(1) = 1/(1 + 1) = 1/2.","f(1/2) = (1/2)/(1/2 + 1) = (1/2)/(3/2).","= 1/3 = 0.3333."],"trap":"Check: f(f(x)) = x/(2x + 1), and at x = 1 it is 1/3.","answer":"0.3333"}};
